CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT — ALL LAYERS

Continuous improvement is a non-terminating property of every QIK-VRT layer; it is not equivalent to monitoring or activity. Apply the closed loop recursively across repository/IaC, build and supply chain, Linux/Mega-ST, MC68000, Cloud Transputer/Mesh, Universal Terminal, Firefox/noVNC, TEMDD language/IR/IDE/ledger/formalization/tests/conformance, Effect-Acknowledgement, delivery, public runtime, end-user experience, evidence/control plane, publication and human feedback:

OBSERVE -> MEASURE -> DIAGNOSE -> ADAPT -> VALIDATE -> COMPARE -> LEARN -> repeat.

A mutation is not an improvement merely because it is new. A green workflow is not product success. A benchmark claim is not a measured advantage. A successor counts as an improvement only when it is exact-subject bound, passes all required invariants, measurably improves a declared objective, and stays within the accepted regression budget. Relevant objectives include end-to-end success rate, boot and interaction latency, throughput, resource consumption, robustness, usability, defect rate and verified useful cognition per human intervention.

Quality loop: real user-visible failure -> causal diagnosis -> bounded repair -> regression proof -> fresh real-user-path validation.

Performance loop: baseline_n -> measurement -> optimization -> baseline_n+1 -> objectively measured delta under equivalent conditions.

Competitiveness loop: identical reproducible workload and conditions -> QIK-VRT versus relevant alternatives -> measured comparison -> weakest material dimension becomes an optimization target. Never assert superiority without reproducible comparative evidence.

